  "historical_output_text": " A common weather pattern is the monsoon. In some parts of the world, like India or Southeast Asia, the weather changes dramatically for a few months each year. The main cause is the movement of air masses.\n\nDuring the spring, the land heats up faster than the ocean. This creates an area of low pressure over the land. Meanwhile, the ocean stays cool and has high pressure. The air moves from the ocean to the land, bringing in moisture from the sea. This causes heavy rainfall and thunderstorms.\n\nAs the days get longer and summer approaches, the sun heats up the land even more. The temperature difference between the land and sea increases. This makes the air flow from the land to the sea, reversing the monsoon winds. The rain stops and the weather becomes hot and dry.\n\nThe timing of when the monsoon arrives and how long it lasts can vary from",
